From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] binder: fix two use-after-frees in binder_free_transaction()

[combined set, 2026-11-06] This file is two consecutive patches of
staging/backport-freezer folded into one. The diff below is the sum of
the two, unchanged; their headers follow in their original order.

Why folded: the second patch changes lines the first one adds, so with
both in the tree "git apply --reverse --check" of the first fails and
scripts/prepare-tree.sh, which uses that check to skip patches that are
already applied, stops on its second run (see the note in "binder:
BINDER_FREEZE and BINDER_GET_FROZEN_INFO ioctls, with the freeze race
fix"). The two separate files remain in
staging/backport-freezer/patches/kernel (0026, 0027).

======== 1/2: "binder: fix UAF in binder_thread_release()" (staging/backport-freezer 0026) ========

When a thread exits, binder_thread_release() walks its transaction stack
and, holding the transaction's lock, detaches every transaction from the
thread: for one the thread was serving it clears t->to_proc, t->to_thread
and t->buffer, then reads t->to_parent and drops the lock.

binder_free_transaction() read t->to_proc without that lock. A second
thread can get there for the same transaction while the first is in the
middle of the steps above: binder_send_failed_reply() walks up
t->from_parent when the thread that should receive a failed reply is
gone, which is the case when the exiting thread had called out from
inside the transaction it was serving and the reply to that nested call
arrives now. If it reads t->to_proc after it was cleared, it takes no
proc lock at all and frees the transaction at once, and
binder_thread_release() goes on to write to_thread, buffer and the lock
word of freed memory (a 96 byte object from kmalloc-128 here).

Read t->to_proc under the transaction lock. binder_free_transaction()
then waits until binder_thread_release() is done with the transaction.
This is also what the comment on the lock in struct binder_transaction
says ("protects @from, @to_proc, and @to_thread").

Derived from upstream:
  114a116aaa5f "binder: fix UAF in binder_thread_release()"

Applies as upstream. The function has two more lines here than in the
upstream context: the outstanding_txns accounting of "binder:
BINDER_FREEZE ioctl" follows directly, and upstream's
binder_txn_latency_free() does not exist in this tree.

Compile-tested (GCC 4.9, lineage_gts210ltexx_defconfig); not run on the
device.

======== 2/2: "binder: fix UAF in binder_free_transaction()" (staging/backport-freezer 0027) ========

binder_free_transaction() takes t->to_proc, drops the transaction lock
and then acquires the inner lock of that process to update its
bookkeeping (t->buffer->transaction and, since "binder: BINDER_FREEZE
ioctl", outstanding_txns and the freeze_wait wakeup). The transaction
holds no reference on the process. When the caller is not that process
itself - binder_send_failed_reply() walking up t->from_parent after the
thread that should get the failed reply has gone - the target process
can finish dying and be freed by binder_deferred_func() in between, and
the inner lock is then taken in freed memory (struct binder_proc is a
kmalloc-512 object here).

Pin the transaction's target thread while its process is used: a
binder_thread holds a temporary reference on its binder_proc from
binder_thread_release() until it is freed, and it is not freed while
its own tmp_ref is raised, so the process cannot go away. t->to_thread
is read under the transaction lock together with t->to_proc; both are
cleared together by binder_thread_release().

A transaction without a target thread needs no pin: it is either one
that binder_thread_release() already detached (to_proc is NULL as well)
or an undelivered one. Those are only freed on behalf of their own
target process, by one of its threads in binder_thread_read() or from
its todo lists in binder_release_work(), where the process is the
caller's or is pinned by binder_deferred_release(). A sender that fails
to queue a transaction frees it with kfree() and does not come here.

Derived from upstream:
  f223d27a546c "binder: fix UAF in binder_free_transaction()"

Applies as upstream on top of the previous patch; thread->tmp_ref is an
atomic_t and binder_thread_dec_tmpref() exists here as in mainline.

Compile-tested (GCC 4.9, lineage_gts210ltexx_defconfig); not run on the
device.
---
 drivers/android/binder.c | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/android/binder.c b/drivers/android/binder.c
index 2450f609..d0db3428 100644
--- a/drivers/android/binder.c
+++ b/drivers/android/binder.c
@@ -2122,7 +2122,20 @@ static struct binder_thread *binder_get_txn_from_and_acq_inner(
 
 static void binder_free_transaction(struct binder_transaction *t)
 {
-	struct binder_proc *target_proc = t->to_proc;
+	struct binder_thread *target_thread;
+	struct binder_proc *target_proc;
+
+	spin_lock(&t->lock);
+	target_proc = t->to_proc;
+	target_thread = t->to_thread;
+	/*
+	 * Pin target_thread to keep target_proc alive. Undelivered
+	 * transactions with !target_thread are safe, as target_proc
+	 * can only be the current context there.
+	 */
+	if (target_thread)
+		atomic_inc(&target_thread->tmp_ref);
+	spin_unlock(&t->lock);
 
 	if (target_proc) {
 		binder_inner_proc_lock(target_proc);
@@ -2136,6 +2149,10 @@ static void binder_free_transaction(struct binder_transaction *t)
 			t->buffer->transaction = NULL;
 		binder_inner_proc_unlock(target_proc);
 	}
+
+	if (target_thread)
+		binder_thread_dec_tmpref(target_thread);
+
 	/*
 	 * If the transaction has no target_proc, then
 	 * t->buffer->transaction has already been cleared.
